1. Test the credentials directly, outside NuGet

powershell
curl.exe -u "YOUR_TELERIK_EMAIL:YOUR_PASSWORD_OR_KEY" https://nuget.telerik.com/v3/index.json
A JSON response means the credentials work, and the problem is how NuGet stored them (see step 3).
A 401 means Telerik is rejecting the login itself.

2. If curl returns 401, use an API key

This is the most common cause. If your Telerik account has two-factor authentication on, or the password has special characters, password login to the feed often fails.

Sign in at telerik.com, then go to Account → Downloads → NuGet Keys.
Generate a key.
Re-add the source with api-key as the username:
powershell
dotnet nuget remove source telerik.com
dotnet nuget add source https://nuget.telerik.com/v3/index.json --name telerik.com --username "api-key" --password "PASTE_KEY_HERE" --store-password-in-clear-text

Put it all on one line to avoid backtick problems. If the key works for curl but the password didn't, you're done.

Also confirm that your Telerik account has an active license or trial that includes the NuGet feed. An expired license gives a 401 too. Your account page shows this under Licenses.

3. If curl works but NuGet still fails, check where the credentials were saved

You ran PowerShell from C:\Windows\system32, which suggests an elevated prompt. If the elevated account differs from the one Visual Studio runs as, the source went into a different user's config. Check:

powershell
dotnet nuget list source --format detailed

and compare against %AppData%\NuGet\NuGet.Config for the account that runs VS. Re-run the add source command from a normal, non-admin PowerShell opened as the same user as VS.

Also check for a nuget.config in C:\ttc2020\CESDEV\ or the solution folder. A solution-level config with a telerik.com entry and different credentials will override the user-level one.

4. Re-test

powershell
dotnet nuget locals all --clear
dotnet package search Telerik.Reporting --source telerik.com

Once that lists versions, restart VS and restore.
